<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Principles of the People</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanmin Zhuyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Principles of the People, nationalism, democracy and the people's livelihood, form the political philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, developed from the early 1900s and set out in lectures of 1924. They became the official doctrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose constitution and national anthem refer to them. Both Nationalists and Communists claimed Sun's legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people.jpg" alt="Three Principles of the People" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 上海同生照相馆 (K.T. Thompson) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sun_Yat_Sen_portrait_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
